  <!-- 5. NOTIFICATION FIX ANDROID -->
  <div class="section reveal" id="s5">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="sec-icon" style="background:#fff1f2;">🔔</div>
      <div>
        <div class="sec-tag">Bug Fix · Android Notifications</div>
        <div class="sec-title"> <span class="index">5</span> Notification Content Not Showing</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-wide">
      <h3>What was broken</h3>
      <p>On Android, push notifications arrived but displayed only the MMUN app icon — no message preview, no sender name, no title. The root cause was a missing <code>notification</code> payload block in our Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) requests. We were sending only a <code>data</code> payload, which Android handles silently in the background — the OS never surfaces its content to the user.</p>
      <p>iOS was unaffected because APNs handles data-only payloads differently. The fix adds a proper <code>notification</code> block with <code>title</code>, <code>body</code>, and Android-specific channel configuration to every outgoing FCM message.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-grid">
      <div class="ba-box ba-before">
        <div class="ba-label">Before</div>
        <p>Android showed only the app icon. No sender name, no message content, no title. Users had to open the app to know what the notification was about.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ba-box ba-after">
        <div class="ba-label">After</div>
        <p>Notifications now show sender name as title, message preview as body, with correct channel, sound, and vibration. Fully visible on lock screen and notification shade.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chip-row">
      <span class="chip chip-pink">Android only</span>
      <span class="chip chip-purple">FCM fix</span>
      <span class="chip chip-green">Bug fixed</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 6. VIDEO STREAMING -->
  <div class="section reveal" id="s6">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="sec-icon" style="background:#f0fdf4;">🎬</div>
      <div>
        <div class="sec-tag">Performance · Media</div>
        <div class="sec-title"> <span class="index">6</span> Improved Video Streaming</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-wide">
      <h3>What changed and why</h3>
      <p>Previously all video elements in the feed loaded and attempted to play simultaneously — regardless of whether they were on screen. With multiple videos this caused severe memory pressure, excessive data usage, and constant stuttering because the browser was decoding multiple streams at once.</p>
      <p>We rewrote our media loading strategy using the <strong>Intersection Observer API</strong> correctly. A video only begins loading its source when it enters the viewport (100px pre-fetch buffer), and pauses or unloads when it scrolls out of view. At any moment, only one or two videos are active.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon" style="background:#ccfbf1;">👁</div>
        <h3>Intersection Observer</h3>
        <p>Each video is individually observed. At 20% viewport visibility loading begins. At 0% visibility the source is cleared and memory freed immediately.</p>
        <div class="card-detail">threshold: [0, 0.2] · rootMargin: 100px</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon" style="background:#dcfce7;">📉</div>
        <h3>Data & memory savings</h3>
        <p>Mobile data users no longer pre-load videos they never watch. Estimated savings on a typical feed scroll are 60–75% versus the old eager-load approach.</p>
        <div class="card-detail">~60–75% less data on video feeds</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chip-row">
      <span class="chip chip-green">Lazy loading</span>
      <span class="chip chip-purple">Intersection Observer</span>
      <span class="chip chip-pink">Data saver</span>
    </div>
  </div>

